Both routes trust `req.params.id` alone, so any authenticated user can update or cancel another account's/order's row by guessing IDs. Fix: scope every `UPDATE` by the caller's own `account_id` (from `req.user`, set by the `sessionUser` middleware in `auth.js`), and treat "0 rows updated" as a 404 rather than returning an empty result.

**`routes/accounts.js`** — an account can only modify itself, so scope by `req.user.accountId`:
```js
router.patch("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET display_name = $1, billing_email = $2 WHERE id = $3 AND id = $4 RETURNING *",
    [req.body.display_name, req.body.billing_email, req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row.length) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});

